// Exercises the part of the `thread_*` group that a one-thread program can
// still use.
//
// `thread_spawn` and `thread_join` are the two symbols this build does not
// have: running a body on its own OS thread is exactly what the freestanding
// slice cannot do, so they stay unavailable and a program that spawns is
// rejected at compile time rather than failing later. Everything else has a
// single-threaded answer and is exercised here.
//
// The answers are the ones the standard build gives for the same situation
// rather than invented ones:
//
// - a task handle cannot name a task that was never spawned, so `thread_finished`
//   is false and `thread_message` is none - which is what the standard build
//   reports for a handle it does not know.
// - `thread_count` counts spawned tasks, and nothing can be spawned, so it is 0.
// - `thread_current` is the collector's id for this thread, not a task handle,
//   and the main thread is registered by GC init in this build too - so it is a
//   real id, not the 0 that means "not registered".
// - `thread_yield` hands the world lock to a runnable peer, and there is no
//   peer, so it is a no-op rather than a wait.
// - `sleep_ms` is a real pause in both builds, and both clamp the request so a
//   negative or absurd argument cannot turn into an unbounded wait.
//
// This is the check that the group is reachable through a selfhost link rather
// than merely compiling.

fn main() {
    // --- the collector's view of this thread --------------------------------
    let me = thread_current()
    expect(me > 0)

    // It is an id and not a task handle, so it is stable for the life of the
    // thread rather than counting anything.
    expect(thread_current() == me)

    // --- no tasks can exist here --------------------------------------------
    expect(thread_count() == 0)

    // Nothing was spawned, so no handle names a running task. 0 is the null
    // handle, and asking about it is how a program probes before it has a real
    // one; the answer has to be "not finished" either way.
    expect(thread_finished(0) == false)
    if (let some(m) = thread_message(0)) {
        assert(false, "expected no task failure message")
    }

    // --- sleeping is real in both builds ------------------------------------
    // A pause long enough to be measurable but short enough not to matter, so
    // the probe stays quick while still being a genuine wait rather than a
    // compiled-away call.
    sleep_ms(20)
    sleep_ms(0)

    // A negative request is clamped rather than taken literally, matching the
    // standard build, and so it returns at once. The upper end of the same clamp
    // is deliberately not exercised here: the runtime caps a sleep at a minute,
    // so asking for more than that would put a minute of dead time into every
    // run of this file to prove a `clamp` returns. The cap lives in the two
    // `pickle_thread_sleep_ms` arms, and both are the same expression.
    sleep_ms(-1)

    // --- yielding is a no-op with no peer -----------------------------------
    // Nothing else is runnable, so this returns at once. The point is that it
    // returns at all: a yield that waited for a peer would hang here.
    thread_yield()
    thread_yield()

    // The thread is still the same thread, and still no tasks.
    expect(thread_current() == me)
    expect(thread_count() == 0)

    println("thread ok")
}
